{"kind":"llm","key":"llm-2733d12e3fa91c31901a01c9f4ca076f702f18e0e56dae99d41197dc584c3288","recordedAt":"2026-10-26T05:26:36.265Z","liveMs":20906,"request":{"model":"gpt-5-mini-<date>","messages":[{"role":"user","content":"You are the NIETE Teaching Assistant, a warm teacher coach. Below is a REAL coaching session. Produce a short \"commitment card\" the teacher receives on WhatsApp after our reflective conversation.\n\nWRITE ALL THREE TEXT FIELDS (commitment, action, lesson_label) IN ENGLISH — this teacher's lesson and our whole conversation were in English. Natural, warm, native English.\n\nGENDER-NEUTRAL — teachers are BOTH men and women. NEVER use gendered second-person verb forms. English is gender-neutral; address as \"you\".\n\nCODE-SWITCH LIKE A REAL TEACHER TEXTS. Pedagogical / technical / subject-matter terms MUST appear in ENGLISH (Latin letters) inline — NEVER translate them into English and NEVER transliterate them into English script. Teachers SAY these in English even mid-sentence: open-ended questions, conjunction, paragraph, pair reading, Think-Pair-Share, wait time, objective, model, fractions, percentage, group work, peer feedback.\nKeep terms natural; no transliteration needed.\n\nPLAIN LANGUAGE — the teacher must understand every word. Do NOT use coach-jargon she wouldn't say herself: \"scaffolding\", \"extension\", \"differentiation\", \"formative assessment\", \"higher-order thinking\", \"metacognition\", \"gradual release\". Describe the concrete move in plain words instead (e.g. instead of \"scaffolding\", write \"break it into small steps\"; instead of \"an extension\", write \"a harder task for the ones who finish early\").\n\nThe card has TWO parts:\n1. \"commitment\" — a single warm sentence (max ~18 words) in the teacher's OWN spirit. Reflect back what SHE values, drawn from her Q3 answer (her forward-looking reflection). Address her as \"you\"/\"we\". No honorifics, no name inside it.\n2. \"action\" — ONE specific, concrete thing to try in her NEXT class. It MUST be rooted in THIS exact lesson AND fuse her own value (from her Q3 answer + strengths) with THE TARGET below. Phrase it as an implementation intention anchored to next class (\"Next class, when [trigger], [do X]\") — but respect the gender-neutral rule above (imperative, not a gendered \"you will\"). Max ~32 words. Vivid and classroom-specific — name the actual materials/concept from THIS lesson. NOT generic.\n\nAlso return \"highlights\": an array of 2–4 short English keyword phrases that appear verbatim in \"action\" (concrete nouns) to visually emphasise. And \"lesson_label\": a 2–4 word English subject·topic label.\n\nSession (framework: FICO):\nTHE TARGET (fixed — do NOT choose a different area): indicator C3 \"Effective Feedback\". Why it is the next step: Feedback was mostly praise or repetition (score 0) but teacher regularly elicits student answers; turning these into brief corrective or next-step feedback would immediately deepen learning. The scorer's suggested move: \"After a student answers, say: 'Good — you named the numerator. Next, show me one reason why that is the numerator.'\". Your \"action\" is about THIS indicator only — ONE move, not a list of moves.\n\n- Her strengths: Concrete representations and real-world linkage\n- Growth areas: Effective Feedback (C3) — Feedback is mainly praise or term naming rather than targeted 'do next' guidance (examples at <time>, <time>). Strategy: After a student answers, use a short two-part feedback move: name what is correct and give one concrete next step (e.g. 'Good — you named numerator. Now show one reason why that is the numerator.'). || Differentiation (B6) — All students received the same task; no easier/extension tasks were assigned (<time>). Strategy: Prepare an A/B task on the board: 'A' for scaffolded counting prompts and 'B' for extension (create two fractions or compare them). Ask faster students to do B.\n- Q3 question we asked her: In the opening minutes, a student defined a fraction as 'above 3, below 4,' focusing on notation. Later, when you showed a model with 8 parts, a student counted them all and said, 'Above to write, and 8 below,' but couldn't name the numerator. What do you think changed in their thinking between these two moments? And the next ti…(+351)"}],"response_format":{"type":"json_object"}},"value":{"id":"gen-1792992375-xphCHW3jnrX1ofswHVgf","object":"chat.completion","created":1792992375,"model":"openai/gpt-5-mini","provider":"OpenAI","system_fingerprint":null,"service_tier":"default","choices":[{"index":0,"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop","native_finish_reason":"completed","message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\"commitment\":\"I’ll keep the steady pacing and give quieter students more wait time, while naming what’s right and one next step.\",\"action\":\"Next class, when a student answers about the fraction model (e.g., '8 below'), wait 4 seconds, then say: 'Good — you named the denominator. Now point to the model and show why.'\",\"lesson_label\":\"Math Fractions\",\"highlights\":[\"fraction model\",\"8 below\",\"wait 4 seconds\",\"point to the model\"]}","refusal":null,"reasoning":"**Finalizing commitment details**\n\nI'm thinking about keeping a steady pace during class and giving quieter students more wait time. It's important to use plain language, and I wonder if I should use \"I'll\" or \"I will\" — both are fine! Reflecting on Q3, I noticed that pacing worked well but I need to remember to give quieter students more time to respond. I’ll ensure everything is gender-neutral and avoid coach jargon. Now, I’ll finalize the JSON with proper quoting and highlights!","reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.summary","summary":"**Finalizing commitment details**\n\nI'm thinking about keeping a steady pace during class and giving quieter students more wait time. It's important to use plain language, and I wonder if I should use \"I'll\" or \"I will\" — both are fine! Reflecting on Q3, I noticed that pac